From e4024e29df90cef0cc3f9c741ae21b9e99f53efa Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: MarsTechHAN <hansh-sz@hotmail.com>
Date: Wed, 23 Sep 2026 23:31:05 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 5/5] [CuTe, SM80] Low-overhead DenseBlockMask launches, 8-warp
 SM8x backward, tests

- Launch cache for SM8x DenseBlockMaskTorch forward: the first call with a
  given signature (shapes, strides, dtypes, alignment, mask_mod object, aux
  metadata) goes through _flash_attn_fwd and registers the compiled kernel
  and its static launch arguments; later calls only allocate outputs and
  launch (host time ~94 us -> ~30 us). Results are bit-identical.
- flash_attn_func: with a DenseBlockMaskTorch and no gradient needed, call
  _flash_attn_fwd directly instead of going through autograd.Function.
- SM8x backward, hdim > 64: 8 warps with atom layouts (2, 4, 4) instead of
  the SM120 setting (4 warps, (4, 4, 4)): dense backward 21.9 -> 18.5 ms at
  S=16k, H=8, D=128 on RTX 4090 (SDPA/FA2: 18.0 ms). The dQ/dK/dV
  postprocess now uses the main kernel's thread count on SM8x, as the fp32
  accumulator layout follows its MMA thread partition. Block-sparse
  backward always uses single-stage Q/dO buffers.
- Tests: bit-exactness of lists / DenseBlockMaskTorch vs the dense kernel
  with an equivalent mask_mod (forward O/LSE, backward dK/dV), dense-mask
  forward/backward vs fp32 reference (uint8 codes and bool + per-column
  partial flags), launch cache.
